    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done by adding the interface under the IS-IS protocol configuration\\n- The equivalent would be to list each interface under `protocols isis`\\n- The loopback interface in Juniper is typically named `lo0` (with unit numbers)\\n- The `.0` suffix is added for physical interfaces and `.1` for loopback by convention\\n- Unlike Huawei's single command, Juniper requires separate statements for each interface\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done by adding the interface under the IS-IS protocol configuration\\n- The equivalent would be to list each interface under `protocols isis`\\n- The loopback interface in Juniper is typically named `lo0` (with unit numbers)\\n- The `.0` suffix is added for physical interfaces and `.1` for loopback by convention\\n- Unlike Huawei's single command, Juniper requires separate statements for each interface\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done by adding the interface under the IS-IS protocol configuration\\n- The equivalent would be to list each interface under `protocols isis`\\n- The loopback interface in Juniper is typically named `lo0` (with unit numbers)\\n- The `.0` suffix is added for physical interfaces and `.1` for loopback by convention\\n- Unlike Huawei's single command, Juniper requires separate statements for each interface\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.1'}]]": 1,
    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name> level 1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface <interface-name> level 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: Replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0`, `ge-2/0/0`, or `lo0.1` for the Loopback1 interface) when applying this command to specific interfaces. The Juniper equivalent enables IS-IS on the interface for level 1 only, matching the Huawei `isis enable 1` command which enables IS-IS process 1 on the interface.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name> level 1'}]]": 1,
    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei command `isis enable 1` enables IS-IS (process 1) on an interface. In Juniper:\\n1. We use `set protocols isis interface` to enable IS-IS on interfaces\\n2. Juniper uses different interface naming:\\n   - `GigabitEthernet1/0/0` becomes `ge-1/0/0.0`\\n   - `GigabitEthernet2/0/0` becomes `ge-2/0/0.0`\\n   - `LoopBack1` becomes `lo0.1`\\n3. The IS-IS process ID (1 in Huawei) isn't specified in Juniper's basic interface enablement\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei command `isis enable 1` enables IS-IS (process 1) on an interface. In Juniper:\\n1. We use `set protocols isis interface` to enable IS-IS on interfaces\\n2. Juniper uses different interface naming:\\n   - `GigabitEthernet1/0/0` becomes `ge-1/0/0.0`\\n   - `GigabitEthernet2/0/0` becomes `ge-2/0/0.0`\\n   - `LoopBack1` becomes `lo0.1`\\n3. The IS-IS process ID (1 in Huawei) isn't specified in Juniper's basic interface enablement\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei command `isis enable 1` enables IS-IS (process 1) on an interface. In Juniper:\\n1. We use `set protocols isis interface` to enable IS-IS on interfaces\\n2. Juniper uses different interface naming:\\n   - `GigabitEthernet1/0/0` becomes `ge-1/0/0.0`\\n   - `GigabitEthernet2/0/0` becomes `ge-2/0/0.0`\\n   - `LoopBack1` becomes `lo0.1`\\n3. The IS-IS process ID (1 in Huawei) isn't specified in Juniper's basic interface enablement\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.1'}]]": 1,
